## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to blend streaming and batch processing with a practical ML feature store, including contracts, versioning, lineage, and schema evolution—common in large tech orgs.

## Key Concepts
- Lakehouse (Iceberg) with feature tables
- Versioned feature registry and per-record data contracts
- Real-time (Flink) vs batch (Spark) feature computation
- Idempotent upserts and late-data handling
- End-to-end data lineage and monitoring

## Code Example
```javascript
function validateRecord(record, schema) {
  // naive contract: required keys and types
  for (const [k, t] of Object.entries(schema.fields)) {
    if (!(k in record)) return { ok: false, error: `missing ${k}` };
    if (typeof record[k] !== t) return { ok: false, error: `${k} type` };
  }
  return { ok: true };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you isolate feature namespaces for different teams and enforce RBAC?
- How would you test schema evolution with backward-compat migrations?

## Why This Is Asked

Data governance for multiple data sources with contracts ensures safety, compatibility, and faster onboarding of teams at scale.

## Key Concepts

- Data contracts and schema registry
- Versioned Iceberg schemas and per-source isolation
- Idempotent upserts and SCD2 history
- Contract testing and rollback strategies

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-contract check: validate record against contract version
function validate(record, contract) {
  // check required fields
  for (const f of contract.required) {
    if (!(f in record)) return false;
  }
  // type checks
  for (const [k, t] of Object.entries(contract.types)) {
    if (typeof record[k] !== t) return false;
  }
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you detect contract drift across sources, and what actions would you trigger?
- How would you handle data already ingested when a contract is updated—rollback, versioning, or retroactive validation?

## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses the ability to design multi-source ingestion pipelines with basic deduplication and schema handling for an entry-level data engineering position.

## Key Concepts
- Change data capture (CDC) from MongoDB
- Batch ingestion from SFTP
- Canonical schema design vs. source schema
- Idempotent writes and deduplication strategies
- Lightweight testing for data contracts and schema drift

## Code Example
```javascript
function enforceContract(rec) {
  const required = ['customer_id', 'event_ts', 'value'];
  const missing = required.filter(field => rec[field] == null);
  if (missing.length > 0) {
    throw new Error(`Missing required fields: ${missing.join(', ')}`);
  }
  if (typeof rec.value !== 'number') {
    throw new Error('Value must be numeric');
  }
  return rec;
}
```
